<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alentejo</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alentejo is a region of southern Portugal between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagus">Tagus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algarve">Algarve</a>, a land of plains, cork oaks, olive groves and wheat fields. Inhabited in antiquity by peoples such as the Cynetes and Celtici, it was part of Roman Lusitania and later of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, and was taken by Portugal in the 12th and 13th centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evora">Évora</a> and Beja are its main historic towns.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alentejo.jpg" alt="Alentejo" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Urgup-tur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_do_Alentejo_em_Portugal.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
